{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CggCxri1mhZKezgNhirJv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Setup.** For d⁸ octahedral, the ground state is ³A₂(F), so ν₁ = 10Dq. The two higher spin-allowed bands come from the ³T₁(F) and ³T₁(P) states, which mix through a 2×2 matrix. I took the matrix elements from memory of the Tanabe–Sugano treatment and did not re-derive them. Energies are measured from the ³A₂ ground state at −12Dq:\n\n- ³T₁(F) diagonal: 6Dq\n- ³T₁(P) diagonal: 15B\n- Off-diagonal: 4B\n\nThe matrix eigenvalues, shifted by +12Dq, give the transition energies:\n\nν₂,₃ = 12Dq + (6Dq + 15B)/2 ∓ ½·√[(15B − 6Dq)² + 64B²]\n\nAdding and subtracting the two roots gives:\n\n- ν₂ + ν₃ = 30Dq + 15B\n- ν₃ − ν₂ = √[(15B − 6Dq)² + 64B²]\n\nC does not enter these spin-allowed energies, so C/B = 4.5 only gives C = 4.5B.\n\n**Solution.**\n\n1. The sum gives 30Dq + 15B = 17 500 + 27 600 = 45 100 cm⁻¹, so B = 3007 − 2Dq.\n2. The difference must equal 27 600 − 17 500 = 10 100 cm⁻¹.\n3. Solving these two equations numerically gives Dq ≈ 1055 cm⁻¹ and B ≈ 897 cm⁻¹.\n4. Check on ν₁: 10Dq = 10 550 cm⁻¹, against 10 600 observed. This is a good match.\n5. If Dq is instead fixed at 1060 cm⁻¹ from ν₁, the sum equation gives B ≈ 887 cm⁻¹. That value reproduces ν₃ − ν₂ as 9 916 cm⁻¹, against 10 100 observed. The two-band fit above is therefore the better one.\n\n**Result:** Dq ≈ 1055 cm⁻¹ (10Dq ≈ 10 550 cm⁻¹) and B ≈ 897 cm⁻¹. With C/B = 4.5, C ≈ 4040 cm⁻¹.\n\n{\"Dq\": 1055, \"B\": 897, \"C\": 4037, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"Fit of the d8 ³T₁(F)/³T₁(P) 2x2 matrix to ν2 and ν3, with ν1 = 10Dq as a consistency check; C = 4.5B.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":857,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":857,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"